  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nYet another warm day across most of northern lower Michigan... \ndriving by southwest winds out ahead of a slow south moving cold \nfront currently cutting across the tip of the mitt. Even despite \nquite a few clouds this morning, seeing widespread readings in the \n60s and 70s for most south of the big bridge. Post-frontal east \nwinds, thicker clouds, and even a few light showers are keeping \neastern upper considerably cooler...with most areas up that way in \nthe 50s.\n\nStraits cold front will be temporarily halted on its journey south \ntonight as weak wave of low pressure rides east along it. More rapid \nsouthern movement of that cold front expected Tuesday afternoon as \nthat low exits stage right. More northern stream influences the \nsecond half of this week into the weekend will return our \ntemperatures to much more normal levels for the middle of October. \n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nShower and thunderstorm evolution tonight/Tuesday, followed by \ntemperature trends and addressing any additional shower potential \nfor the rest of the period.\n\nDetails: \n\nAs mentioned, front will essentially stall across northern Michigan \ntonight into Tuesday morning as weak shortwave trough and attendant \njust as weak surface low ride east along it. Strongest surge of \nisentropic upglide/Fgen response and upper jet support looks to \nalign across the tip of the mitt into eastern upper later tonight \ninto early Tuesday. Expect showers to be more organized and \nwidespread in that area, with showers more scattered the further \nsouth one goes. Enough elevated instability to promote some embedded \nthunderstorms as well, but definitely not expecting anything too \nintense (maybe some small hail under the most intense updrafts?). In \ngeneral, looking at quarter to half inch rain totals for those \nnorthern areas...likely less to the south. Locally heavier totals up \nto and perhaps over an inch are possible, especially in areas that \nsee some repeated thunderstorms. Welcome rains indeed, especially \nfor those across eastern upper Michigan. Still very mild tonight, \nwith perhaps some areas down near M-55 not getting out of the 60s \n(warmer than normal high temperatures). That stalling front will let \nsouthern areas warm up quite significantly again on Tuesday, with \ncurrent thermal progs supporting temperatures again topping \n70...especially south of M-72. Considerably cooler further north, \nwith much of eastern upper likely stuck in the 50s. \n\nReality sets in a bit heading into later Tuesday and Wednesday as \nthe front gets a boot south with passage of that surface low. While \nthis will end the rain threat, northwest winds will continue to \nusher in cooler conditions, with highs by Wednesday much closer to \nwere they should be in the 50s and 60s.\n\nGuidance has come in much better agreement sending a rather sharp \nnorthern stream shortwave trough crossing the area Thursday night \ninto Friday. Now, with that said, this wave is slated to exit much \nfaster and does not look nearly as robust as some guidance was \nsuggesting just yesterday. Forcing is rather impressive however, \nsupporting good chances for showers by Thursday night, with quick \nhitting shot of some impressive cold air advection supporting lake \nshowers on Friday. That cold air will support highs only topping out \nin the lower to middle 50s Friday.\n\nCurrent trends support a mostly dry weekend and beginning of next \nweek as large area of high pressure builds into and through the \nregion. Still chilly on Saturday, with again highs mostly in the \nlower to middle 50s. Temperatures look to moderate Sunday and Monday \nin developing return flow, with current progs supporting highs back \nup into the 60s by Monday.",
